  "objects": [
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*As defined in* the existing chipdb convention (`chipdb/rlvgl-chips-esp/db/chips/*.yaml`); *used without modification* for the file-naming + lookup contract. SiLabs-specific schema fields are §5 froze",
        "term": "Chip YAML"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 122,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:chip yaml",
      "text": "A document under `chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/db/chips/<chip>.yaml` describing one Silicon Labs SoC's memory map, clock tree, peripheral instances, and IO routing tables. The file stem is the canonical chip id."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*As defined in* the existing chipdb convention (`chipdb/rlvgl-chips-esp/db/boards/*.yaml`); *used without modification*.",
        "term": "Board YAML"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 123,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:board yaml",
      "text": "A document under `chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/db/boards/<board>.yaml` naming a chip and a pin-by-pin assignment of the chip's peripheral signals to physical package pins."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter; first reference in repo `db/chips/EFM32GG11.yaml`.",
        "term": "Chip id"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 124,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:chip id",
      "text": "The `name:` field of a chip YAML. SHOULD match the silkscreen / orderable-part-number stem (e.g. `EFM32GG11`, `EFR32MG24`, `EFR32BG22`). MUST be unique within the crate."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter.",
        "term": "Board id"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 125,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:board id",
      "text": "The `name:` field of a board YAML. SHOULD match the vendor's marketed board name with vendor-prefix stripped (e.g. `EFM32GG11_STK` for the SLSTK3701A Giant Gecko 11 starter kit; `BRD4002A` for the WPK mainboard)."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*Owned by* each upstream PAC's maintainer; *cited* by chip YAML.",
        "term": "PAC crate"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 126,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:pac crate",
      "text": "The Rust `svd2rust`-derived peripheral access crate named by a chip YAML's `pac_crate:` field. CHIPS-SILABS does not generate or vendor PACs; it generates code that calls them."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter; mirrors the ESP set defined in `src/bin/creator/bsp/espressif/templates/`.",
        "term": "6-file emission set"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 127,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:6-file emission set",
      "text": "The fixed output produced by `rlvgl-creator bsp from-yaml --vendor silabs`: `mod.rs`, `pac.rs`, `clocks.rs`, `io_mux.rs`, `peripherals.rs`, `board.rs`. See §6. *Linker scripts (`memory.x`, `chip.x`)* are NOT in this set at v0 — they are §11 deferred work."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*As defined in* per-family Silicon Labs RMs (EFM32GG11 RM §11, EFR32MG24 RM §8); *cited*.",
        "term": "CMU"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 128,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:cmu",
      "text": "Clock Management Unit. The Silicon Labs peripheral that owns clock source selection, branch enables, and per-peripheral clock gating across Series 0/1/2. CMU register layout differs between Series 0/1 (single-register CMU_HFPERCLKEN0) and Series 2 (`CMU.CLKEN0/CLKEN1` + per-peripheral `CMU.<periph>CLKCTRL`)."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*As defined in* per-family RMs; *cited*.",
        "term": "HFCLK / HFXO / HFRCO"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 129,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:hfclk / hfxo / hfrco",
      "text": "High-frequency clock tree roots. HFXO is the external crystal; HFRCO is the internal RC oscillator; HFCLK is the post-mux high-frequency root that feeds the CPU and most peripherals. Series 2 renames these to SYSCLK / HFXO / HFRCODPLL."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*As defined in* per-family RMs; *cited*.",
        "term": "EM mode"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 130,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:em mode",
      "text": "Energy Mode. The Silicon Labs naming for low-power states: EM0 (run), EM1 (sleep), EM2 (deep sleep, retains state), EM3 (stop), EM4 (off/hibernate). CHIPS-SILABS template output MUST leave the part in EM0 at the end of `init()`."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*As defined in* per-family RMs (EFM32GG11 RM §32.5); *cited*.",
        "term": "GPIO ROUTE / ROUTELOC"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 131,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:gpio route / routeloc",
      "text": "Series 0/1 peripheral pin routing mechanism: each peripheral has a `<PERIPH>_ROUTEPEN` enable register and one or more `<PERIPH>_ROUTELOC0/1` location-select registers that pick which physical pin a peripheral signal exits on. Distinct from Series 2 DPLLOC."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "*As defined in* Series 2 family RMs (EFR32MG24 RM §24.5 \"GPIO pin routing\"); *cited*. **TBD: needs Silabs EFR32xG2x RM cite** for the exact register-block naming (some Series 2 parts use `GPIO_USART0_",
        "term": "DPLLOC (digital peripheral allocator / pin allocator)"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 132,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:dplloc (digital peripheral allocator / pin allocator)",
      "text": "Series 2 replacement for ROUTELOC: each peripheral signal (e.g. `USART0_TX`) is allocated to a specific GPIO via `GPIO.<PERIPH>ROUTE.<signal>ROUTE` registers, with `GPIO.<PERIPH>ROUTE.ROUTEEN.<signal>PEN` gating the connection. The §10 reconciliation notes that DPLLOC is NOT a \"matrix\" in the ESP sense — there is no opaque signal-id table; instead each peripheral has a *named* per-signal route reg"
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter; mirrors `chipdb/rlvgl-chips-esp/db/chips/esp32c3.yaml` lines 39–108.",
        "term": "System gate"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 133,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:system gate",
      "text": "A row in a chip YAML's `clock_tree.system_gates:` map: `{ clk_en_reg, clk_en_field, rst_reg, rst_field, [clk_sel_reg, clk_sel_field] }`. Drives the `clocks.rs.jinja` template's per-peripheral clock-enable + reset-pulse emission. Mirrors the ESP / Nordic shape; SiLabs-specific differences are noted in §10."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter; mirrors ESP/Nordic shape.",
        "term": "Peripheral instance"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 134,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:peripheral instance",
      "text": "A row in a chip YAML's `peripherals:` map: `{ class, instance, base, irq, signals: [...] }`. The `class` is a frozen enum (§5); `instance` is the PAC peripheral name; `signals` lists the pin-routable signals each peripheral exposes."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter.",
        "term": "Signal"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 135,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:signal",
      "text": "One entry in a peripheral's `signals:` list: `{ role, direction, [route_reg, route_field, pen_field] }`. The `role` is peripheral-specific (`tx`, `rx`, `sck`, `mosi`, etc.). The route fields name DPLLOC route registers on Series 2 or ROUTELOC slots on Series 0/1. The route-field naming convention is one of the §10 reconciliation decisions because it differs between Series 0/1 and Series 2."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter.",
        "term": "Pin route"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 136,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:pin route",
      "text": "An entry in a board YAML's `pins:` list: `{ port, pin, signal, peripheral, direction, [pull], [drive_strength], [label] }`. SiLabs GPIO addressing is `(port, pin)` (e.g. `(PortA, 5)`), distinct from ESP's flat `gpio:` integer and Nordic's `(port, pin)` packed `psel` u32. The schema MUST keep `port` and `pin` as separate scalar fields."
    },
    {
      "attrs": {
        "relationship": "Owned by this chapter.",
        "term": "Console"
      },
      "doc": "chipdb/rlvgl-chips-silabs/docs/CHIPS-SILABS-00-CONCEPTS.md",
      "family": "chips-silabs",
      "kind": "term",
      "line": 137,
      "obj_id": "chips-silabs:term:console",
      "text": "The peripheral instance that the generated `peripherals.rs` brings up as a UART debug console, named by a board YAML's `console:` block. SiLabs Series 0/1 uses `USARTn`, `LEUARTn`, or `UART0`; Series 2 uses `USARTn` or `EUSARTn`. The `console.peripheral:` value MUST be one of the chip's frozen peripheral instances."
    },
